Title: The Creole
Order: 6
Production Code: 1x06

Radio play, Part 6 of 6. Adapts the second half of Part 4 of the novella. Running time about 16 minutes. CONTENT NOTE: period racial language appears in Tom Grant's account, as in the source; it is kept verbatim.

INT. RICHMOND - THE MARINE COFFEE-HOUSE - DAY

AMB: the coffee-house, quiet, a clock, a ship's bell outside

MUSIC: BED IN - the single low string, a swell under it, steady

Tom Grant has the whole room.

GRANT
(slow, a man going back)
The attack began just about twilight in the evening. Apprehending a squall, I had commanded the second mate to order all hands on deck, to take in sail.

SFX: the coffee-house dissolving into surf, a ship under sail, wind in rigging

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. THE CREOLE - THE DECK - TWILIGHT

AMB: an open sea, a rising wind, canvas cracking, a low swell, the creak of rigging, a sky darkening to the west

SFX: halyards, blocks, the hull shouldering a wave

SFX: a mate's call, "lay aloft", sailors scrambling up shrouds

GRANT (V.O.)
A few minutes before this I had seen Madison's head above the hatchway, looking out upon the white-capped waves at the leeward. I think I never saw him look more good-natured.

SFX: a hatch cover lifted a hand's breadth, a man's slow breath, the hatch lowered

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I stood just about midship, on the larboard side. The captain was pacing the quarter-deck on the starboard side, in company with Mr. Jameson, the owner of most of the slaves on board. Both were armed.

SFX: two men's boots on a deck, pacing, turning, pacing

Captain Clarke and Mr. Jameson walk and talk, comfortably, a little bored.

CAPTAIN CLARKE
(off, idle, a captain at ease)
Squall coming. We'll have her snug before it breaks.

MR. JAMESON
(off)
She's carried well this far.

SFX: a pistol, a single shot, flat across the water

MUSIC: STING - a hard, shattering chord

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I had just told the men to lay aloft, and was looking to see my orders obeyed, when I heard the discharge of a pistol on the starboard side; and turning suddenly around, the very deck seemed covered with fiends from the pit.

SFX: a hatch flung back, a roar of feet on deck, chains clanking free, a dozen men at once

AMB: the whole ship, a storm of running, shouting, wind

MUSIC: BED IN - a hand drum, driving, and wild strings under it

SFX: a body falling, a sailor's shout cut short, a heavy handspike striking a deck rail

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The nineteen negroes were all on deck, with their broken fetters in their hands, rushing in all directions. I put my hand quickly in my pocket to draw out my jack-knife.

SFX: a blow, a man's grunt, a body slamming to the planks

SFX: the sudden silence of a man knocked senseless

AMB: the wind, the sea, the creak of rigging, the world far away

MUSIC: BED OUT

SFX: a long ragged breath, a man coming back to himself, wet breathing, the sea

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
When I came to myself, which I did in a few minutes, I suppose, for it was yet quite light, there was not a white man on deck. The sailors were all aloft in the rigging, and dared not come down.

SFX: the rigging, a scatter of frightened voices overhead, ropes creaking under weight

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Captain Clarke and Mr. Jameson lay stretched on the quarter-deck, both dying, while Madison himself stood at the helm unhurt.

SFX: two men's laboured breathing, close together, slowing

SFX: a ship's wheel under a steady hand, the spokes creaking, a leather glove

Madison Washington has the wheel. He does not raise his voice.

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I was completely weakened by the loss of blood, but it was a little too much for me, even in my prostrate condition, to see our good brig commanded by a black murderer. So I called out to the men to come down and take the ship, or die in the attempt. Suiting the action to the word, I started aft.

SFX: Grant's unsteady boots across the deck, stumbling, dragging

GRANT
(shouting, hoarse)
You murderous villain!

SFX: a violent shove, a body flung back against rail, a grunt

MADISON
(quiet, no anger)
Stand back, sir.

SFX: Grant, breathing, backing, a hand on the rail

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I started towards Madison again. The rascal now told me to stand back.

MADISON
(calm, exact, formal, and utterly terrifying because of it)
Sir, your life is in my hands. I could have killed you a dozen times over during this last half hour, and could kill you now. You call me a black murderer. I am not a murderer.

AMB: the wind, the sea, the rigging, the whole ship holding still

MADISON (CONT'D)
God is my witness that LIBERTY, not malice, is the motive for this night's work. I have done no more to those dead men yonder than they would have done to me in like circumstances.

MUSIC: BED IN - low cello, one note, slow

MADISON (CONT'D)
We have struck for our freedom; and if a true man's heart be in you, you will honor us for the deed. We have done that which you applaud your fathers for doing; and if we are murderers, so were they.

SFX: Grant, breathing, hard, a moment of absolute stillness

GRANT (V.O.)
I felt little disposition to reply to this impudent speech. By heaven, it disarmed me. The fellow loomed up before me. I forgot his blackness in the dignity of his manner, and the eloquence of his speech. It seemed as if the souls of both the great dead, whose names he bore, had entered him.

MUSIC: BED OUT

MADISON
(turning his head aloft, a voice that carries over the wind)
Men! The battle is over. Your captain is dead. I have complete command of this vessel. All resistance to my authority will be in vain.

SFX: the rigging creaking, a hushed whisper among the sailors overhead

MADISON (CONT'D)
My men have won their liberty with no other weapons but their own BROKEN FETTERS.

SFX: nineteen men, on a deck, a sound like a single indrawn breath

MADISON (CONT'D)
We are nineteen in number. We do not thirst for your blood. We demand only our rightful freedom. Do not flatter yourselves that I am ignorant of chart or compass. I know both. We are now only about sixty miles from Nassau. Come down, and do your duty. Land us in Nassau, and not a hair of your heads shall be hurt.

SFX: a man's boots, running at the deck, a swinging handspike

GRANT
(shouting)
Stay where you are, men!

SFX: a heavy stick swung, a body thrown between two others, a blow absorbed

MADISON
(sharp, once)
No!

SFX: the handspike dropped, rolling on planks, the man stopped by a hand on his chest

GRANT (V.O.)
A sturdy black fellow ran at me with a handspike, and would have split my head open, but for the interference of Madison, who darted between me and the blow.

MADISON
(to Grant, low and exact)
I know what you are up to. You want to navigate this brig into a slave port, where you would have us all hanged. But you'll miss it. Before this brig shall touch a slave-cursed shore while I am on board, I will myself put a match to the magazine, and blow her, and be blown with her, into a thousand fragments.

SFX: a flint struck, a spark, a flame in a hand, held

The Tinder-Box sound comes back for one beat: a spark struck, held up to the dark, then closed in a fist.

MADISON (CONT'D)
(quieter)
Now I have saved your life twice within these last twenty minutes. For when you lay helpless on deck, my men were about to kill you. I held them in check. And if you now, seeing I am your friend and not your enemy, persist in your resistance to my authority, I give you fair warning: you shall die.

MUSIC: STING - one long low note

SFX: four muskets cocked in a row, a sailor's whimper overhead

MADISON (CONT'D)
(to the rigging, in a tone from which there is no appeal)
Come down.

SFX: ropes creaking, boots on shrouds, a slow descent

GRANT (V.O.)
Saying this to me, he cast a glance into the rigging where the terror-stricken sailors were clinging, like so many frightened monkeys, and commanded them to come down, in a tone from which there was no appeal; for four men stood by with muskets in hand, ready at the word of command to shoot them down.

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings and drum, coiling

AMB: the wind rising, a hiss in the west, the first fat drops of rain

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I now became satisfied that resistance was out of the question; that my best policy was to put the brig into Nassau, and secure the assistance of the American consul at that port. By this time the apprehended squall had burst upon us.

SFX: thunder, sudden, close, a flash visible in the sound

MUSIC: BED OUT - strings cut by the storm

EXT. THE CREOLE - THE STORM - NIGHT

AMB: a full gale, wind roaring in rigging, rain on a hundred square yards of canvas, a ship in huge seas, timber groaning on every beam, thunder, thunder

SFX: a sea breaking over the bow, water pouring across the deck

SFX: a wave taking the quarter-deck, two bodies carried off with the water, the roll of the ship

GRANT (V.O.)
The wind howled furiously. The ocean was white with foam, which, on account of the darkness, we could see only by the quick flashes of lightning that darted occasionally from the angry sky. All was alarm and confusion.

SFX: women's cries from below, a hundred and thirty voices at once, a hatch rattling

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Hideous cries came up from the slave women. Above the roaring billows a succession of heavy thunder rolled along, swelling the terrific din. Owing to the great darkness, and a sudden shift of the wind, we found ourselves in the trough of the sea. When shipping a heavy sea over the starboard bow, the bodies of the captain and Mr. Jameson were washed overboard.

SFX: the hull heeling, a long creak, a sea breaking again

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
For awhile we had dearer interests to look after than slave property. A more savage thunder-gust never swept the ocean. Our brig rolled and creaked as if every bolt would be started, and every thread of oakum would be pressed out of the seams.

GRANT
(shouting into the wind)
To the pumps! To the pumps!

SFX: the sailors, not moving, gripping the rigging, fear, a whimper

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(a short breath)
But not a sailor would quit his grasp. Fortunately this squall soon passed over, or we must have been food for sharks.

AMB: the storm, at its height, nothing else in the world

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, a drum, the cello in the clear, steady

SFX: a ship's wheel, hand over hand, the spokes, the compass bowl rocking in the binnacle

Madison Washington hasn't moved. We hear the wheel turning, turning, holding, correcting.

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
During all the storm, Madison stood firmly at the helm, his keen eye fixed upon the binnacle. He was not indifferent to the dreadful hurricane; yet he met it with the equanimity of an old sailor. He was silent but not agitated.

SFX: the storm lessening, the gale loosening its grip, the rain easing

AMB: the sea, slowly quieting, a big slow swell, the wind thinning

MUSIC: BED OUT

SFX: a hush, a long drawn breath, the wheel steady in the swell

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The first words he uttered after the storm had slightly subsided were characteristic of the man.

MADISON
(quietly, to Grant, as if musing)
Mr. mate, you cannot write the bloody laws of slavery on those restless billows. The ocean, if not the land, is free.

AMB: the sea, the wind, the dark, a great quiet

MUSIC: BED IN - a single sustained cello note, no drum

GRANT (V.O.)
I confess, gentlemen, I felt myself in the presence of a superior man; one who, had he been a white man, I would have followed willingly and gladly in any honorable enterprise. Our difference of color was the only ground for difference of action.

SFX: the ship's bell, once, over a calm sea

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
It was not that his principles were wrong in the abstract; for they are the principles of 1776. But I could not bring myself to recognize their application to one whom I deemed my inferior.

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. NASSAU HARBOUR - NIGHT

AMB: a harbour at night, water on a hull, a lantern swinging, a distant bell, a gull

SFX: a ship being warped in to a wharf, ropes thrown, a hull nudging timber

GRANT (V.O.)
But to my story. What happened now is soon told. Two hours after the frightful tempest had spent itself, we were plump at the wharf in Nassau.

SFX: boots running on planks, a man's urgent voice, a door slammed

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I sent two of our men immediately to our consul with a statement of facts, requesting his interference in our behalf. What he did, or whether he did anything, I don't know; but, by order of the authorities, a company of black soldiers came on board, for the purpose, as they said, of protecting the property.

SFX: marching boots on a gangplank, a drill, a sharp order, muskets grounded on a deck

The Nassau Soldiers stand at attention in the lantern light.

GRANT
(quick, to the sergeant)
These people are property! By the laws of Virginia and the laws of the United States, the slaves on board are as much property as the barrels of flour in the hold. Keep them aboard!

THE SERGEANT
(formal, level, unmoved)
Our instructions, sir, are to protect property. We do not recognize persons as property.

SFX: a long silence, the lantern creaking on its hook

GRANT (V.O.)
At this the stupid blockheads showed their ivory, rolled up their white eyes in horror, as if the idea of putting men on a footing with merchandise were revolting to their humanity.

MUSIC: BED IN - the drum, slow, returning, a hymn-like pulse under it

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
When these instructions were understood among the negroes, it was impossible for us to keep them on board. They deliberately gathered up their baggage before our eyes, and, against our remonstrances, poured through the gangway.

SFX: feet on the gangway, bundles, chains falling away onto the deck, a long metallic tumble of broken irons

SFX: a hundred voices, low, hushed, and then not hushed

AMB: the wharf, the harbour at night, the gathering sound of a crowd, lanterns coming one by one

SFX: a shout from the wharf, a cheer starting, a bell somewhere

MUSIC: BED IN - the whole company, a hymn rising, drums joining, strings swelling, the first full-hearted music of the drama

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Formed themselves into a procession on the wharf, bid farewell to all on board, and, uttering the wildest shouts of exultation, they marched, amidst the deafening cheers of a multitude of sympathizing spectators.

SFX: the crowd, a multitude of voices, pouring through the harbour night, a cheer going up and up

SFX: the marching feet, steady, a long column, a drum in step with them

SFX: the chains, not a link of them, anywhere

AMB: the crowd, a harbour, a night of celebration

GRANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Under the triumphant leadership of their heroic chief and deliverer, MADISON WASHINGTON.

MUSIC: STING - a single triumphant chord, held, cut off at the moment of the cheer's peak

AMB: the crowd, cut to silence

INT. SOUND STAGE - BLACK

AMB: absolute quiet, a room tone just barely there

MUSIC: BED IN - the single low string, now warm, with a distant wave under it

The Narrator is quiet now, and the wind has gone out of the drama.

NARRATOR
Glimpses of this great character are all that can now be presented. We do not know where he walked from that wharf, or what he said to the first free night.

SFX: a page turned, once, near

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
But we know he was there. And we know that the sea, if not the land, was free.

MUSIC: BED OUT - to nothing

AMB: the sea, very far, very calm, fading to silence

MUSIC: STING - one clear low note, then nothing at all
